        "points": [
          "The Harappan civilisation represents an early phase of urbanisation in the Indian subcontinent.",
          "After the decline of the Harappan urban system, urban life was greatly reduced for a long period.",
          "The new phase of urbanisation began in the 1st millennium BCE.",
          "This new phase is often called the Second Urbanisation of India.",
          "The Ganga plains were an important centre of the new urbanisation.",
          "Parts of the Indus basin and neighbouring regions also participated in the process.",
          "Archaeological excavations provide important evidence of ancient urban centres.",
          "Late Vedic, Buddhist and Jain literature contains references to urban centres.",
          "Janapadas were territorial political units associated with settled groups or clans.",
          "Mahājanapadas were larger political units that emerged as states expanded or merged.",
          "Trade networks connected janapadas and encouraged economic interaction.",
          "Fertile land and agricultural productivity supported population and surplus.",
          "Surplus food could support people engaged in crafts, trade and administration.",
          "Iron tools were among the technological developments associated with this period.",
          "Specialised crafts included pottery, weaving and metalworking.",
          "Cities could become centres of administration, markets, crafts and cultural activity.",
          "States required systems of administration, revenue collection and defence.",
          "Fortifications could strengthen important settlements and capitals.",
          "Some ancient communities developed collective political traditions such as gana-sanghas.",
          "Ancient political participation should not automatically be equated with modern universal democracy.",
          "The varṇa framework and jāti groups were parts of complex social organisation.",
          "Different regions of the subcontinent experienced change in different ways.",
          "Coins, inscriptions, buildings, pottery and tools are valuable historical evidence.",
          "Historians compare multiple sources to build more reliable interpretations.",
          "The Second Urbanisation was a connected transformation involving economy, society, politics, technology and geography."
        ]
