        "type": "chapterSummary",
        "title": "📝 Chapter Summary",
        "text": "New Beginnings: Cities and States explains a major transformation in the history of the Indian subcontinent. After the decline of the Harappan urban system, urban life was greatly reduced for a long period. During the 1st millennium BCE, a new phase of urbanisation developed, especially in the Ganga plains and neighbouring regions. Archaeological discoveries and ancient literature help historians study these new urban centres.\n\nRegional groups became associated with territories known as janapadas. As states expanded and trade networks connected regions, larger political units called mahājanapadas emerged. Different forms of political organisation developed, including kingdoms and collective traditions associated with gana-sanghas. The growth of cities and states was supported by agriculture, surplus production, crafts, trade, technology, routes, administration and defence.\n\nThe chapter also introduces changing social organisation, including the varṇa framework and the many jāti groups found in historical society. Developments were not identical across all regions, so the history of the subcontinent must be studied with attention to regional diversity.\n\nMost importantly, the chapter teaches us how interconnected historical change can be. Agricultural production could create surplus; surplus could support crafts and trade; trade could encourage towns; growing towns and territories required administration and defence; and political organisation could influence urban growth. Historians reconstruct these processes by comparing archaeology, texts, coins, inscriptions, buildings and other evidence."
